  "problem": {
    "original_text": "Nonlinear Dynamics: Duffing Oscillator (Chaos + Bifurcation + Poincare) Topic: ODEs, nonlinear dynamics, numerical integration, chaos diagnostics. Problem: Consider the forced, damped Duffing oscillator x'' + 2zeta x' + x + beta x^3 = gamma cos(omega t), with parameters zeta = 0.15, beta = 1, omega = 1. Treat gamma as a control parameter. Tasks (plot required in at least three distinct steps): (a) Integrate the ODE numerically for gamma in {0.2, 0.3, 0.37, 0.45} over a long time horizon. Discard transients and plot the time series x(t). Use the plots to classify motion as periodic, quasi-periodic, or chaotic-looking. (b) For the same gamma values, plot phase portraits (x(t), x'(t)). Identify attractors (limit cycles vs complex/strange-attractor evidence) and explain how damping/forcing/nonlinearity affect geometry. (c) Construct a Poincare section by sampling (x, x') at times t_k = 2pi k / omega. Plot the resulting points for each gamma and compare the structure (single point, finite set, closed curve, fractal-like scatter). (d) Sweep gamma from 0.10 to 0.60 in small increments. For each gamma, collect post-transient Poincare samples and plot a bifurcation diagram of x-values vs gamma. Locate period-doubling cascades and periodic windows. Optional (still plot-heavy): (e) Estimate the largest Lyapunov exponent lambda(gamma) numerically and plot lambda vs gamma to corroborate chaotic regimes.",
    "normalized_text": "Duffing oscillator x'' + 2\\zeta x' + x + \\beta x^3 = \\gamma \\cos(\\omega t) with \\zeta=0.15, \\beta=1, \\omega=1. Sweep gamma and produce time series, phase portraits, Poincare sections sampled at t_k=2\\pi k/\\omega, a bifurcation diagram for gamma in [0.10,0.60], and optionally largest Lyapunov exponent estimates.",
